  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer today and dry through Monday. \n\nThe surface high overhead will drift off to our east and then \nsoutheast today to the Mid Atlantic region. While the high will\ncontinue to support dry weather, we will see southerly flow \n(WAA) develop which will aid in boosting temperatures across the\nregion. Highs today will be found in the 60s across WNY, with \nupper 50s to near 60F across the North Country. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chance of showers Tuesday. Well above normal \ntemperatures expected for much of the upcoming work week. \n\nA low pressure moving out of the Colorado Rockies and a warm \nfrontal segment will extend east from this system on Tuesday \nwhich may introduce a few showers to the region. In the wake of \nthe warm front, southerly flow (WAA) will push temperature well \ninto the 60s to low 70s Tuesday, and the upper 60s to mid 70s on\nWednesday. While the overall evolution of this system still \nremains uncertain, it does appear it will be dry on Wednesday. \nAfter that, the cold front will inch its way east with unsettle \nweather (showers) Thursday and then maybe some showers lingering\nthrough the end of the week.",
